-- title:
--   Re-pairing two corners of one vertex keeps a map planar
-- statement:
--   For permutations $r,f$ of a finite set $A$, let $\mathrm{cyc}$ count cycles (fixed points included) and $\mathrm{comp}(r,f)$ count the classes of the equivalence relation generated by both. The pair is a planar map if
--   $$
--   \mathrm{cyc}(r)+\mathrm{cyc}(f)+\mathrm{cyc}(rf)=|A|+2\,\mathrm{comp}(r,f),
--   $$
--   i.e. equality holds in the Euler bound. Let $r$ be a fixed-point-free involution with a colouring constant along $rf$. Take darts $a,b$ of different colours with $\mathrm{color}(r a)=\mathrm{color}(b)$ and $\mathrm{color}(a)=\mathrm{color}(r b)$, $r a\ne b$, and $r a$, $b$ in the same cycle of $rf$. If $(r,f)$ is planar, then so is $(\mathrm{rePair}(r,a,b),f)$, where
--   $$
--   \mathrm{rePair}(r,a,b)=(r a\ \ b)\,r\,(r a\ \ b)
--   $$
--   pairs $a$ with $b$ and $ra$ with $rb$.
--
--   No embedding or Jordan curve theorem is used; planarity is the Euler count.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/GroupTheory/Hyperbolic/Planarity.lean#L153-L239

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_TorsionFreeHyperbolic
import Definitions.Def_OAI_GroupTheory_Hyperbolic_MatrixRank
import Definitions.Def_OAI_GroupTheory_Hyperbolic_Incidence
import Definitions.Def_OAI_GroupTheory_Hyperbolic_Presentation
